            <ArticleTitle>Intraradicular, intervertebral disc herniation. A case report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1028-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">A case of intraradicular, intervertebral disc herniation is reported.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">A rupture of en L5-S1 intervertebral disc into the left S1 nerve root is described.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SUMMARY OF BACKGROUND DATA" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">A review of the literature revealed five reported cases with some possible causes of this extremely rare phenomenon.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The diagnosis was made at the time of surgery during exploration of the L5-S1 disc space. The S1 nerve root was found to be adherent to the underlying disc space. Before surgery, this condition was believed to represent a large extradural and extraradicular disc herniation.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The patient's pain was alleviated almost immediately after surgery, but the patient was left with some residual sensory loss in the leg.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">An unusual case of intraradicular, intervertebral disc herniation is presented.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
